+Renaming a variable should not change an arithmetic answer. Yet a model may answer 4
+because a variable is named \texttt{four}, even when its equations give 6. We ask whether
+writing the calculation before answering reduces such errors. We extend
+\citeauthor{kudo2026faithful}'s study of how language models compute intermediate values
+during chain-of-thought reasoning. Our problems add misleading variable names while keeping
+the equations and correct answers fixed. Across \NUM{n-models} models and three sets of worked
+examples, we compare direct answers with reasoning chains that restate and evaluate each equation.
+Without written steps, renaming changes accuracy or the frequency of name-suggested answers
+in \NUM{n-value-models} models. With steps, the increase in name-suggested answers over ordinary
+names falls within $\pm\NUM{eq-delta}$ percentage points in \NUM{eq-cot-equivalent} of
+\NUM{eq-cot-cells} comparisons. Classifiers trained to recover values from model states do
+not establish why: their association with errors depends on one low-accuracy model, and
+they perform poorly on its generated steps even after training on such steps. The experiments
+support a behavioral reduction while leaving its internal cause unresolved.
 \end{abstract}
 
